import BlueprintPage from '../components/BlueprintPage.astro'; const sections=[
{title:'Information and patient rights',paragraphs:['People seeking care should be able to ask about confidentiality, consent, records, fees, options, follow-up, and when another service is needed. Before a consultation, ask who will provide the service, their stated professional role, what the appointment is intended to cover and what the fee includes. You can ask for explanations in language you understand and take time to consider options.','Informed consent is an ongoing conversation. Ask what a proposed assessment or treatment involves, its expected purpose, possible risks, alternatives and what might happen if you choose to wait or decline. You may raise questions during care and ask how decisions will be reviewed. For children, clinicians should explain the process to caregivers and include the child in a developmentally appropriate way.']},
{title:'Confidentiality, consent and records',paragraphs:['Ask how information is recorded, who may access it, how long it is retained and when information might need to be shared for safety or legal reasons. If family members, schools or another clinician are involved, ask what information would be shared and what consent is required. You can ask to clarify a record or understand how to request a copy through the clinic’s process. Read the Mind Grace <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> and <a href="/consent">consent information</a>.','Confidentiality does not mean that every situation can be kept private under all circumstances. The clinician should explain relevant limits and discuss next steps when safety is a concern. If you do not understand what is being asked, pause and ask for clarification before agreeing.']},
{title:'Respectful, collaborative care',paragraphs:['Care should take a person’s background, language, identity, family context and preferences seriously. You can share what matters to you, what has been difficult in previous care and what would make an appointment more accessible. If you need an interpreter, a support person, an accommodation or a different way to communicate, ask whether arrangements can be made.','You may ask about the clinician’s qualifications and scope, how a proposed approach fits your concern and what happens if the service is not the right fit. If you feel a question or recommendation is unclear, request another explanation. For a second opinion or referral, ask what records would help and how to keep current care coordinated.']},
{title:'Educational limits and urgent help',paragraphs:['This website provides general information and does not replace diagnosis, treatment or emergency support. Online checklists and developmental tools do not provide a clinical diagnosis unless the page explicitly describes a validated instrument and its use; the Aasha developmental check-in is a discussion aid, not a diagnostic or validated screening test. A clinician should consider the individual’s circumstances.','For immediate danger, call 112 or go to the nearest emergency department; routine clinic channels are not monitored for emergency response. Mind Grace is not an emergency service. For routine support, read <a href="/process">what to expect</a>, review <a href="/fees">fees</a> and use <a href="/contact">the published contact options</a>.']}
];
